#: source/language/string-interpolation.md:1
msgid "String interpolation"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/string-interpolation.md:3
msgid ""
"String interpolation is a language feature where a [string](./types.md#type-"
"string), [path](./types.md#type-path), or [attribute name](./types.md#type-"
"attrs) can contain expressions enclosed in `${ }` (dollar-sign with curly "
"brackets)."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/string-interpolation.md:5
msgid ""
"Such a construct is called _interpolated string_, and the expression inside "
"is an [interpolated expression](#interpolated-expression)."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/string-interpolation.md:11
msgid "**Syntax**"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/string-interpolation.md:13
msgid "_interpolation_element_ → `${` _expression_ `}`"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/string-interpolation.md:15
msgid "Examples"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/string-interpolation.md:17
msgid "String"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/string-interpolation.md:19
#: source/language/string-interpolation.md:93
msgid "Rather than writing"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/string-interpolation.md:21
msgid ""
"```nix\n"
"\"--with-freetype2-library=\" + freetype + \"/lib\"\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/string-interpolation.md:25
msgid ""
"(where `freetype` is a [derivation expression](../glossary.md#gloss-"
"derivation-expression)), you can instead write"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/string-interpolation.md:29
msgid ""
"```nix\n"
"\"--with-freetype2-library=${freetype}/lib\"\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/string-interpolation.md:33
msgid "The latter is automatically translated to the former."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/string-interpolation.md:35
msgid ""
"A more complicated example (from the Nix expression for [Qt](http://"
"www.trolltech.com/products/qt)):"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/string-interpolation.md:37
msgid ""
"```nix\n"
"configureFlags = \"\n"
"  -system-zlib -system-libpng -system-libjpeg\n"
"  ${if openglSupport then \"-dlopen-opengl\n"
"    -L${mesa}/lib -I${mesa}/include\n"
"    -L${libXmu}/lib -I${libXmu}/include\" else \"\"}\n"
"  ${if threadSupport then \"-thread\" else \"-no-thread\"}\n"
"\";\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/string-interpolation.md:47
msgid ""
"Note that Nix expressions and strings can be arbitrarily nested; in this "
"case the outer string contains various interpolated expressions that "
"themselves contain strings (e.g., `\"-thread\"`), some of which in turn "
"contain interpolated expressions (e.g., `${mesa}`)."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/string-interpolation.md:50
msgid ""
"To write a literal `${` in an regular string, escape it with a backslash "
"(`\\`)."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/string-interpolation.md:52
#: source/language/string-interpolation.md:62
#: source/language/string-interpolation.md:74
#: source/language/string-interpolation.md:120
#: source/language/string-interpolation.md:131
#: source/language/string-interpolation.md:159
#: source/language/string-interpolation.md:175
#: source/language/string-interpolation.md:188
#: source/language/string-interpolation.md:204
#: source/language/string-interpolation.md:217
#: source/language/string-interpolation.md:230
msgid "**Example**"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/string-interpolation.md:54
msgid ""
"```nix\n"
"\"echo \\${PATH}\"\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/string-interpolation.md:58
msgid ""
"    \"echo ${PATH}\"\n"
"    "
msgstr ""

#: source/language/string-interpolation.md:60
msgid ""
"To write a literal `${` in an indented string, escape it with two single "
"quotes (`''`)."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/string-interpolation.md:70
msgid ""
"    \"echo ${PATH}\\n\"\n"
"    "
msgstr ""

#: source/language/string-interpolation.md:72
msgid "`$${` can be written literally in any string."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/string-interpolation.md:76
msgid ""
"In Make, `$` in file names or recipes is represented as `$$`, see [GNU "
"`make`: Basics of Variable Reference](https://www.gnu.org/software/make/"
"manual/html_node/Reference.html#Basics-of-Variable-References). This can be "
"expressed directly in the Nix language strings:"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/string-interpolation.md:87
msgid ""
"    \"MAKEVAR = Hello\\nall:\\n\\t@export BASHVAR=world; echo $(MAKEVAR) $\\$"
"{BASHVAR}\\n\"\n"
"    "
msgstr ""

#: source/language/string-interpolation.md:89
msgid "See the [documentation on strings](./types.md#type-string) for details."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/string-interpolation.md:91
msgid "Path"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/string-interpolation.md:95
msgid ""
"```nix\n"
"./. + \"/\" + foo + \"-\" + bar + \".nix\"\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/string-interpolation.md:99
msgid "or"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/string-interpolation.md:101
msgid ""
"```nix\n"
"./. + \"/${foo}-${bar}.nix\"\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/string-interpolation.md:105
msgid "you can instead write"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/string-interpolation.md:111
msgid "Attribute name"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/string-interpolation.md:118
msgid "Attribute names can be interpolated strings."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/string-interpolation.md:122
msgid ""
"```nix\n"
"let name = \"foo\"; in\n"
"{ ${name} = 123; }\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/string-interpolation.md:129
msgid "Attributes can be selected with interpolated strings."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/string-interpolation.md:133
msgid ""
"```nix\n"
"let name = \"foo\"; in\n"
"{ foo = 123; }.${name}\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/string-interpolation.md:140
msgid "Interpolated expression"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/string-interpolation.md:142
msgid ""
"An expression that is interpolated must evaluate to one of the following:"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/string-interpolation.md:144
msgid "a [string](./types.md#type-string)"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/string-interpolation.md:145
msgid "a [path](./types.md#type-path)"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/string-interpolation.md:146
msgid ""
"an [attribute set](./types.md#type-attrs) that has a `__toString` attribute "
"or an `outPath` attribute"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/string-interpolation.md:148
msgid ""
"`__toString` must be a function that takes the attribute set itself and "
"returns a string"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/string-interpolation.md:149
msgid "`outPath` must be a string"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/string-interpolation.md:151
msgid ""
"This includes [derivation expressions](./derivations.md) or [flake inputs]"
"(../command-ref/new-cli/nix3-flake.md#flake-inputs) (experimental)."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/string-interpolation.md:153
msgid "A string interpolates to itself."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/string-interpolation.md:155
msgid ""
"A path in an interpolated expression is first copied into the Nix store, and "
"the resulting string is the [store path](../store/store-path.md) of the "
"newly created [store object](../store/store-object.md)."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/string-interpolation.md:165
msgid "Reference the empty directory in an interpolated expression:"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/string-interpolation.md:167
msgid ""
"```nix\n"
"\"${./foo}\"\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/string-interpolation.md:171
msgid ""
"    \"/nix/store/2hhl2nz5v0khbn06ys82nrk99aa1xxdw-foo\"\n"
"    "
msgstr ""

#: source/language/string-interpolation.md:173
msgid ""
"A derivation interpolates to the [store path](../store/store-path.md) of its "
"first [output](./derivations.md#attr-outputs)."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/string-interpolation.md:177
msgid ""
"```nix\n"
"let\n"
"  pkgs = import <nixpkgs> {};\n"
"in\n"
"\"${pkgs.hello}\"\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/string-interpolation.md:184
msgid ""
"    \"/nix/store/qnlr7906z0mrl2syrkdbpicffq02nw07-hello-2.12.1\"\n"
"    "
msgstr ""

#: source/language/string-interpolation.md:186
msgid ""
"An attribute set interpolates to the return value of the function in the "
"`__toString` applied to the attribute set itself."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/string-interpolation.md:190
msgid ""
"```nix\n"
"let\n"
"  a = {\n"
"    value = 1;\n"
"    __toString = self: toString (self.value + 1);\n"
"  };\n"
"in\n"
"\"${a}\"\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/string-interpolation.md:200
msgid ""
"    \"2\"\n"
"    "
msgstr ""

#: source/language/string-interpolation.md:202
msgid ""
"An attribute set also interpolates to the value of its `outPath` attribute."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/string-interpolation.md:206
msgid ""
"```nix\n"
"let\n"
"  a = { outPath = \"foo\"; };\n"
"in\n"
"\"${a}\"\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/string-interpolation.md:213
msgid ""
"    \"foo\"\n"
"    "
msgstr ""

#: source/language/string-interpolation.md:215
msgid ""
"If both `__toString` and `outPath` are present in an attribute set, "
"`__toString` takes precedence."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/string-interpolation.md:219
msgid ""
"```nix\n"
"let\n"
"  a = { __toString = _: \"yes\"; outPath = throw \"no\"; };\n"
"in\n"
"\"${a}\"\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/string-interpolation.md:226
msgid ""
"    \"yes\"\n"
"    "
msgstr ""

#: source/language/string-interpolation.md:228
msgid "If neither is present, an error is thrown."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/string-interpolation.md:232
msgid ""
"```nix\n"
"let\n"
"  a = {};\n"
"in\n"
"\"${a}\"\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/string-interpolation.md:239
msgid ""
"    error: cannot coerce a set to a string: { }\n"
"    \n"
"           at «string»:4:2:\n"
"    \n"
"                3| in\n"
"                4| \"${a}\"\n"
"                 |  ^\n"
"    "
msgstr ""
